WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.100
A coastal dune plant stops at a northern limit.

2
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.420
Plants were established about 60 km beyond that limit.

3
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.980
Twelve to fourteen years later, that population had more than tripled.

4
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.060
So the plant can live there.

5
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.980
Why had it never got there?

6
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:21.613
The answer starts with the niche.

7
00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:24.893
Hutchinson gave it this form in 1957.

8
00:00:26.093 --> 00:00:30.733
A niche is the set of conditions and resources in which a population can persist

9
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:31.733
without immigrants.

10
00:00:32.853 --> 00:00:35.713
Temperature and soil moisture are two axes of that space.

11
00:00:36.693 --> 00:00:38.593
Niche space is the space of conditions.

12
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:41.033
Geographic space is the map.

13
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:45.853
A species can be absent from a suitable place because it has not reached it.

14
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.167
How do we test persistence?

15
00:00:49.127 --> 00:00:51.347
We use lambda the finite rate of increase.

16
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:56.007
Lambda is the factor by which a population multiplies in one year.

17
00:00:56.847 --> 00:01:00.187
If lambda is one or more, the population replaces itself.

18
00:01:01.107 --> 00:01:02.667
A sink population is different.

19
00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:06.287
There, deaths exceed births, and immigrants keep it going.

20
00:01:07.107 --> 00:01:10.587
So presence on a map, by itself, does not show where the niche ends.

21
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:13.487
A range edge could have three causes.

22
00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:15.987
Conditions beyond it could be wrong.

23
00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:18.307
That is an abiotic limit.

24
00:01:19.187 --> 00:01:20.567
Other species could be in the way.

25
00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:22.647
That is a biotic limit.

26
00:01:23.407 --> 00:01:25.527
Or the species may simply not have arrived.

27
00:01:26.307 --> 00:01:27.767
That is a dispersal limit.

28
00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:32.287
The direct test is to plant the species beyond the edge, and follow it.

29
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.000
A good test plants the same way inside the range.

30
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:41.880
If the plantings inside fail too, the test cannot single out the edge, and handling

31
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.460
or a bad year may be to blame.

32
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.580
Then cross the planting with neighbour removal.

33
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.120
Clear half the plots, chosen at random, and leave half.

34
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.700
Last, measure the whole life cycle.

35
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.320
A fall in first-year growth does not show that a population cannot replace itself.

36
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.340
Estimate lambda

37
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.310
Now read the result.

38
00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:07.650
If lambda is at least one beyond the range in both neighbour treatments, the species

39
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.650
can persist there.

40
00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.190
Dispersal or colonisation is the likely limit.

41
00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.110
Cross and Eckert drew a similar inference for their dune plant, at one site.

42
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:22.350
If lambda falls below one only where neighbours remain, a biotic limit is

43
00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:27.570
implicated. Suppose lambda falls below one in every treatment beyond the range.

44
00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.750
If the inside controls stay at one or above, conditions beyond the edge are

45
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.950
implicated. Climate is a strong candidate, but enemies and partners need their own

46
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.990
tests. Keep this rule.

47
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:44.170
Absence on a map, by itself, does not show where the niche ends.
